Story Content: {'story': 'On a rainy Tuesday morning, Sarah discovered an old letter hidden in her grandmother’s attic...\n\nSarah eased the flap open. Otis, who had been waiting with his chin on her shoe, sneezed at the dust and bumped the old trunk with his nose. A photograph slipped from beneath its warped lid. It showed her grandmother as a young woman, standing beside someone Sarah didn’t recognize, both of them in muddy shoes. Between them sat a black dog with one ear folded inside out. On the back, in the same blue ink as the letter, were the words: “Tuesday, before the train. I kept my promise.” Sarah looked toward the rain-dark window. Downstairs, Otis gave one short bark.\n\nBehind her, the loose attic door bumped open. The gray cat Grandma always called “the neighbor’s” stepped inside, shook rain from its whiskers, and made for the old trunk as if Sarah’s discovery had inconvenienced its morning nap. Sarah tucked the letter beneath her sweater before the cat could step on it. On the envelope, in the same neat capitals Grandma used on the pantry jars, was a name Sarah hadn’t seen in years.\n\nSarah brushed dust from the envelope. The name was Ruth Bell—her grandmother’s younger sister, the one Sarah had been told left Baltimore before Sarah was born and never came back. The gray cat settled on the photograph, pinning one corner beneath its paw. Sarah lifted it gently and found a small station token underneath, worn smooth at the edges. From downstairs, Otis barked again, then began whining at the attic steps. Sarah closed her hand around the token. “All right,” she whispered, though she wasn’t sure whether she meant the dog or the name.\n\nSarah turned the photograph over again, rubbing her thumb along the words as though the ink might give way to something more. Muddy shoes. A train. A promise her grandmother had kept—or meant to keep. From somewhere below came the scrape of Otis’s claws across the floorboards. The gray cat settled beside the trunk and began washing one paw, entirely unimpressed. Sarah glanced at the open letter. The first line was addressed to the same unfamiliar name on the envelope.\n\nSarah unfolded the letter carefully. The paper crackled, and a faint brown mark spread beneath her thumb. The first line read: “Ruth—if the train has already gone, don’t let them tell you I left alone.” She read it twice. Below, the ink had blurred, but a date remained legible—Tuesday, the same day as the photograph. Otis barked from downstairs, impatient now. Sarah folded the letter around the station token and listened to the rain tapping the roof.\n\nSarah slipped the token into her pocket and started down the attic stairs. The gray cat stayed on the photograph, its tail curled over its paws, as if the people in it had finished being interesting. Otis met Sarah on the landing, then hurried toward the front hall and stopped at the door. She took out the token again. Along its worn rim, almost polished away, was one word: BALTIMORE. Otis whined at the rain-streaked glass. Sarah stood with the letter in one hand and the token in the other, not quite ready to turn the knob.'}
